1. Purpose

This document summarizes our progress on BearBuddy as of October 15, 2026. It covers our current ERD draft, API research, security considerations, and project organization. The goal is to keep track of what we have worked on so far and identify questions we still need to address. Features and technologies discussed in our research are not considered final decisions unless we have confirmed them.

2. Existing Database Design

We have created a draft entity-relationship diagram (ERD) for BearBuddy. Our current diagram includes six entities.

- User: User ID, email, password hash, first name, last name, display name, and account creation date and time.
- Obstacle_Report: Report ID, user ID, location ID, obstacle type, description, reported date and time, expiration date and time, and status.
- Campus_Location: Location ID, name, building, latitude, and longitude.
- Class_Schedule: Schedule ID, user ID, course name, building, room, meeting days, start time, and end time.
- Personal_Event: Event ID, user ID, title, description, type, start time, end time, and location.
- Saved_Account: Saved account ID, user ID, website name, website URL, username, and encrypted password.

Our ERD connects users to their class schedules, personal events, saved accounts, and obstacle reports. Obstacle reports are also connected to campus locations.

Items We Need to Review

As we continue developing our database design, there are a few areas we need to review.

- Naming consistency: Some fields appear to have spelling or formatting issues, including `exprired_at` and `saved_account-id`. We need to confirm the intended names and make them consistent.
- Authentication: We need to determine whether storing a local password hash is necessary if we use UC single sign-on.
- Saved accounts: If we keep the saved-account feature in our project scope, we need to carefully evaluate how those credentials would be protected.
- Obstacle reports: We need to define the available report statuses and determine how and when reports should expire.
- Location relationships: We need to decide whether class schedules and personal events should reference our existing `Campus_Location` entity instead of storing location information separately.

These are issues we have identified in our current draft. We have not necessarily resolved them, and our ERD may change as we review the design.

3. API Research

We have put together an API research document covering possible tools for mapping, navigation, calendars, location services, authentication, and security.

Mapping and Navigation

Our research compares Google Maps Platform and Azure Maps as potential options for BearBuddy's campus navigation features.

We identified Google Maps Platform as an option worth exploring further because its routing capabilities and Navigation SDK could support our navigation goals. However, standard mapping services would not automatically account for obstacles submitted by BearBuddy users. We would need to develop our own application logic to incorporate reports about construction, blocked sidewalks, and other campus conditions.

Before choosing a provider, we still need to look into pricing, React Native compatibility, routing accuracy around UC, API usage limits, API key security, location privacy, and provider requirements.

Current status: Our research recommends evaluating Google Maps Platform further, but we have not documented a final provider selection or an implemented mapping feature.

Calendar APIs

Our research also identifies Google Calendar API and Microsoft Graph Calendar API as possible options for calendar-related features.

Both could be useful if we include calendar integration in BearBuddy. However, our current research does not provide a complete comparison of the two services or establish which one we will use.

Current status: We have identified both APIs for consideration. We still need to compare them before deciding which option, if either, best fits our project.

4. Security and Authentication

Authentication and account security are important considerations for BearBuddy. In our earlier planning, we identified UC single sign-on and technologies such as Microsoft Entra ID, OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect (OIDC), PKCE, and MSAL as areas to research.

We still need to confirm which authentication setup UC supports for this type of project and whether any approvals are required. Our current materials do not establish that a specific configuration has been approved or implemented.

We should not collect or store users' UC passwords. We also need to ensure that API keys, access tokens, and other sensitive credentials are kept out of our GitHub repository.

Current status: Authentication remains an area we need to research. We still need to confirm the supported UC sign-in process and any associated requirements.

